Yes 😄 — both are real issues.

    The “missing r” should be a true empty space, not an underline/dash.

    The poem lost spacing because every character became an inline span with no preserved word spacing logic.

So the fixes are:

    remove .ww-answer-three-gap border styling entirely

    preserve spaces using:

character === " "
  ? "\u00A0"
  : character

inside the poem renderer.

Also:

white-space: pre;

on the line container will stabilise spacing perfectly.

Those two changes will fix it cleanly.

talk me through the first chasnge pls :)

Right now, the script creates a fake “missing letter” slot here:

letter === "r"
  ? `<span class="ww-answer-three-gap"></span>`
  : ""

That inserts this element:

<span class="ww-answer-three-gap"></span>

Then CSS draws the visible line:

.ww-answer-three-gap {
  border-bottom: 2px solid rgba(...);
}

So visually, it becomes:

a b c d e f g _ h i

But what you actually want is:

a b c d e f g   h i

— a true absence. Empty air. Missing.

So the clean fix is:

    keep the slot itself (so spacing/alignment stays correct)

    remove the visible border line entirely

Meaning the “r position” still occupies space in the alphabet row, but renders absolutely nothing.

That creates a much creepier effect because the viewer subconsciously notices something is wrong without seeing a literal blank marker.

